title: "Merge PDF: Join PDF Files into One, in Your Browser"
layout: base.njk
date: 2026-11-07
updated: 2026-11-07
meta_title: "Merge PDF Files Online: Combine PDFs in Order, No Upload"
meta_description: "Combine two or more PDF files into one in your browser. See how many pages each file has, put the files in order and download the merged PDF. Nothing is uploaded, and there is no file limit or watermark."
meta_keywords: "merge pdf, combine pdf, join pdf files, merge pdf online free, combine pdf without upload, merge pdf files into one"
noIconFont: true
faqs:
  - q: "Are my PDFs uploaded anywhere?"
    a: "No. The files are read and joined by your browser with pdf-lib, an open-source PDF library that runs on this page. Nothing is sent to a server, so bank statements, contracts and ID documents stay on your device."
  - q: "Is there a limit on files or pages?"
    a: "There is no set limit and no watermark. The only limit is your device's memory, because the files are held in the browser while they are joined. Most phones and laptops handle dozens of ordinary PDFs without trouble."
  - q: "Why does it say my PDF is password protected?"
    a: "The file is encrypted, and its pages cannot be copied without the password. Open it in a PDF reader with the password, then use Print and choose Save as PDF, which makes an unlocked copy. Merge that copy here."
  - q: "Do links, bookmarks and form fields survive?"
    a: "The pages, text, images and page sizes are copied exactly, and the text stays searchable. Bookmarks from the original files are not carried over, and filled form fields and links that point to other pages may not work in the merged file. Check the result if those matter."
  - q: "Can I merge only some pages of a file?"
    a: "This tool joins whole files. To keep only certain pages, open the PDF in a reader, print those pages with Save as PDF, and merge the smaller file here."
  - q: "Will the merged file be larger than the originals?"
    a: "It is usually close to the size of the files added together. The pages are not compressed again, so their quality does not change."
